1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,176
Look at an elite swimmer on the blocks: broad
shoulders tapering into a narrow

2
00:00:05,176 --> 00:00:05,561
waist.

3
00:00:05,911 --> 00:00:11,067
They look like superheroes, and while they
train with targeted weights on land,

4
00:00:11,067 --> 00:00:14,999
their wide back is forged primarily
against water resistance.

5
00:00:15,349 --> 00:00:20,704
How does moving through water build one of the
most athletic upper bodies in sports?

6
00:00:21,054 --> 00:00:24,716
Today: the biomechanics behind
the swimmer's V-taper,

7
00:00:24,716 --> 00:00:28,649
and how to build that pulling
power, shoulder durability,

8
00:00:28,649 --> 00:00:31,294
and rigid core on land and in the pool.

9
00:00:31,644 --> 00:00:35,783
The swimmer's physique is a survival
adaptation to fluid physics.

10
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:38,842
Water is 800 times denser than air.

11
00:00:39,192 --> 00:00:44,077
To conquer that fluid drag, your body
relies on 5 functional pillars.

12
00:00:44,427 --> 00:00:47,490
Pillar 1: the hull, minimizing form drag.

13
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:50,552
Pillar 2: the wings, your pulling engine.

14
00:00:50,552 --> 00:00:53,834
Pillar 3: the chassis,
locking a rigid core.

15
00:00:53,834 --> 00:00:58,063
Pillar 4: the pivot point,
rebalancing your rotator cuff.

16
00:00:58,063 --> 00:01:03,678
And Pillar 5: the fuel and iron, beating the
cold-water appetite trap with a

17
00:01:03,678 --> 00:01:05,355
targeted gym blueprint.

18
00:01:05,705 --> 00:01:09,310
The first rule of swimming:
fluid drag always wins.

19
00:01:09,661 --> 00:01:16,041
Form drag—the water pushing against your head
and shoulders—causes 85 to 90 percent

20
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:21,053
of total resistance, while skin
friction is only 10 to 15 percent.

21
00:01:21,403 --> 00:01:27,513
The power needed to overcome water drag scales
with velocity cubed: doubling speed

22
00:01:27,513 --> 00:01:29,574
requires 8 times more power.

23
00:01:29,923 --> 00:01:35,811
Reducing frontal surface area saves vastly
more energy than brute pulling force.

24
00:01:36,161 --> 00:01:40,150
In a locked streamline,
surface area drops to 0.

25
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:41,452
13 square meters.

26
00:01:41,802 --> 00:01:46,688
Lifting your head or sagging your hips by
10 degrees doubles that area,

27
00:01:46,688 --> 00:01:48,927
creating an underwater parachute.

28
00:01:49,277 --> 00:01:54,993
Think of a submarine versus a flat barge:
massive power on a flat barge just throws

29
00:01:54,993 --> 00:01:57,170
up spray without moving forward.

30
00:01:57,521 --> 00:02:02,129
Swimmers look tapered because their body
acts like a narrow torpedo hull.

31
00:02:02,479 --> 00:02:05,427
Elite swimmers have positive ape indices,

32
00:02:05,427 --> 00:02:08,516
wingspans 3 to 6 percent
longer than height.

33
00:02:08,866 --> 00:02:13,794
You cannot stretch bones, but you can master
the zero-drag streamline lock.

34
00:02:14,144 --> 00:02:17,579
Stack your dominant hand over the
other, locking the thumb.

35
00:02:17,929 --> 00:02:22,857
Squeeze your upper arms behind your ears,
looking straight down at the pool floor.

36
00:02:23,207 --> 00:02:24,029
The mistake?

37
00:02:24,029 --> 00:02:26,432
Looking forward at the opposite wall,

38
00:02:26,432 --> 00:02:30,162
which drops your hips 15 centimeters
and doubles form drag.

39
00:02:30,511 --> 00:02:36,446
Pillar 2 is the pulling engine that builds the
wide V-taper: the lats, teres major,

40
00:02:36,446 --> 00:02:37,082
and pecs.

41
00:02:37,432 --> 00:02:43,064
Amateurs pull by dropping elbows, sliding hands
through water like a slanted ramp.

42
00:02:43,414 --> 00:02:47,894
Elite swimmers execute an Early
Vertical Forearm, or E-V-F.

43
00:02:48,244 --> 00:02:54,151
Keep your elbow high near the surface while
hinging your forearm down at a 90 to

44
00:02:54,151 --> 00:02:55,391
110-degree angle.

45
00:02:55,741 --> 00:02:59,410
This turns your hand and forearm
into a vertical paddle.

46
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:03,237
The hand produces 85 to 90
percent of thrust,

47
00:03:03,237 --> 00:03:08,528
while the forearm adds 10 to 15 percent
and anchors a column of water.

48
00:03:08,878 --> 00:03:12,526
Think of pole vaulting: your arm
freezes into an anchor,

49
00:03:12,526 --> 00:03:15,022
and your lats vault your torso forward.

50
00:03:15,372 --> 00:03:19,639
Here is the secret of the swimmer's
back: non-spinal muscle growth.

51
00:03:19,989 --> 00:03:26,581
On land, heavy barbell rows apply 3,000 to
7,000 Newtons of spinal compression.

52
00:03:26,931 --> 00:03:32,264
In water, Archimedes' buoyant force unloads
80 to 90 percent of body weight.

53
00:03:32,614 --> 00:03:37,280
Swimmers perform 16,000 arm
rotations weekly—over 1.

54
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:39,569
5 million strokes per year.

55
00:03:39,919 --> 00:03:45,567
Buoyancy unloads your spine, allowing your lats
to absorb massive volume with zero

56
00:03:45,567 --> 00:03:46,724
disc compression.

57
00:03:47,074 --> 00:03:53,111
In the gym, train this with straight-arm cable
pulldowns for 3 sets of 8 to 10 reps.

58
00:03:53,461 --> 00:03:56,903
In the pool, practice front
sculling with a pull buoy,

59
00:03:56,903 --> 00:03:59,093
sweeping forearms in figure eights.

60
00:03:59,443 --> 00:04:00,207
The mistake?

61
00:04:00,207 --> 00:04:05,144
Dropping the elbow early, pushing water down
instead of back and switching off your

62
00:04:05,144 --> 00:04:05,438
lats.

63
00:04:05,788 --> 00:04:09,948
A massive pulling engine is useless
without a rigid chassis.

64
00:04:10,298 --> 00:04:12,941
Your center of buoyancy sits
in your lungs,

65
00:04:12,941 --> 00:04:16,485
while your center of mass sits lower
in your hips and legs.

66
00:04:16,835 --> 00:04:19,279
Because lungs float while legs sink,

67
00:04:19,279 --> 00:04:22,979
gravity and buoyancy create a
rotational sinking torque.

68
00:04:23,329 --> 00:04:27,012
Relaxing your abs drops your lower
back into a banana shape,

69
00:04:27,012 --> 00:04:29,366
dragging your hips down like an anchor.

70
00:04:29,716 --> 00:04:35,249
Think of pushing a solid steel tube through
water versus a cooked spaghetti noodle:

71
00:04:35,249 --> 00:04:38,740
a rigid cylinder glides, while a
soft noodle buckles.

72
00:04:39,090 --> 00:04:43,101
Swimmers build a rigid cylinder through
the hollow body position.

73
00:04:43,451 --> 00:04:48,727
Contracting your transversus abdominis and
obliques pulls your pelvis into a

74
00:04:48,727 --> 00:04:50,235
posterior pelvic tilt.

75
00:04:50,585 --> 00:04:54,039
This flattens your lumbar curve and
squeezes your glutes,

76
00:04:54,039 --> 00:04:56,302
lifting your hips to the surface line.

77
00:04:56,652 --> 00:05:02,056
Alternating strokes also produce rotational
torque that twists hips sideways,

78
00:05:02,056 --> 00:05:04,481
known as torso yaw or fish-tailing.

79
00:05:04,831 --> 00:05:08,038
Targeted core training
eliminates torso yaw,

80
00:05:08,038 --> 00:05:11,530
directly improving breakout
speed and race times.

81
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:17,046
Build this chassis with the gymnastic
hollow body hold: lie flat,

82
00:05:17,046 --> 00:05:22,760
press your lower back down, and hold for 3
to 4 sets of 30 to 45 seconds.

83
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:29,030
To resist rotational twisting, perform the
standing cable Pallof press for 3 sets of

84
00:05:29,030 --> 00:05:30,214
10 reps per side.

85
00:05:30,564 --> 00:05:31,356
The mistake?

86
00:05:31,356 --> 00:05:33,730
Arching your lower back off the floor,

87
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:36,836
which disengages your deep abs
and sinks your hips.

88
00:05:37,186 --> 00:05:41,922
Now we must confront the dark side of the
swimmer's physique: swimmer's shoulder.

89
00:05:42,272 --> 00:05:48,746
Between 40 and 91 percent of competitive
swimmers suffer from chronic shoulder pain,

90
00:05:48,746 --> 00:05:53,621
and 69 percent show M-R-I confirmed
supraspinatus tendon damage.

91
00:05:53,971 --> 00:05:56,254
The culprit is muscular imbalance.

92
00:05:56,604 --> 00:05:59,394
Your internal rotators—the lats, pecs,

93
00:05:59,394 --> 00:06:03,687
and subscapularis—grow immensely
powerful with every stroke.

94
00:06:04,037 --> 00:06:10,030
Meanwhile, your external rotators—the
infraspinatus and teres minor—receive minimal

95
00:06:10,030 --> 00:06:10,672
stimulus.

96
00:06:11,021 --> 00:06:17,253
In healthy athletes, external rotator
strength is 66 to 75 percent of internal

97
00:06:17,253 --> 00:06:18,594
rotator strength.

98
00:06:18,945 --> 00:06:23,873
In swimmers, that ratio frequently
plunges below 50 to 60 percent.

99
00:06:24,223 --> 00:06:30,196
Think of an 800-horsepower racing engine on a
driveshaft held by loose rubber bands.

100
00:06:30,546 --> 00:06:35,604
Your lats have massive power, but weak
external rotators cannot keep the ball

101
00:06:35,604 --> 00:06:37,095
centered in the socket.

102
00:06:37,445 --> 00:06:41,625
Overpowered internal rotators pull
the humeral head forward,

103
00:06:41,625 --> 00:06:45,189
pinching the supraspinatus
tendon on every recovery.

104
00:06:45,539 --> 00:06:49,700
Furthermore, the serratus anterior
fatigues during long swims,

105
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:53,134
stopping your shoulder blade
from upwardly rotating.

106
00:06:53,484 --> 00:06:59,737
Bulletproof your shoulders with three drills:
side-lying external rotations with a

107
00:06:59,737 --> 00:07:04,860
light 1 to 2-kilogram dumbbell or band
for 3 sets of 12 to 15 reps,

108
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:09,456
prone Y-T-W raises on an incline
bench for 3 sets of 8 reps,

109
00:07:09,456 --> 00:07:12,620
and serratus wall slides with a mini-band.

110
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:13,752
The mistake?

111
00:07:13,752 --> 00:07:18,026
Shrugging your upper traps; pull shoulder
blades down and back instead.

112
00:07:18,376 --> 00:07:24,764
Pillar 5 solves the great swimming mystery: why
do swimmers struggle with body fat

113
00:07:24,764 --> 00:07:28,381
despite burning 600 to 1,000
calories per hour?

114
00:07:28,731 --> 00:07:32,336
The cause is fluid thermal
physics and endocrinology.

115
00:07:32,687 --> 00:07:37,444
Water conducts heat away from your body
24 times faster than air.

116
00:07:37,794 --> 00:07:44,202
Even in heated 26-degree pools, water is 7
degrees colder than skin and 11 degrees

117
00:07:44,202 --> 00:07:45,901
colder than your core.

118
00:07:46,251 --> 00:07:51,742
To prevent hypothermia, your nervous system
constricts peripheral blood vessels,

119
00:07:51,742 --> 00:07:53,504
driving warm blood inward.

120
00:07:53,854 --> 00:07:59,821
Climbing out into room air triggers a survival
reflex: a massive surge of active

121
00:07:59,821 --> 00:08:02,473
ghrelin, the primary hunger hormone.

122
00:08:02,823 --> 00:08:10,374
Exercising in cool water drives a 44 percent
spike in spontaneous calorie intake

123
00:08:10,374 --> 00:08:17,180
compared to neutral conditions—877 calories
consumed versus 608 calories.

124
00:08:17,530 --> 00:08:20,403
Swimmers burn hundreds of calories,

125
00:08:20,403 --> 00:08:26,149
but unknowingly overconsume 500 to 1,000
calories immediately afterward.

126
00:08:26,499 --> 00:08:32,180
To beat this trap, take a hot shower and drink a
warm beverage post-swim to quickly

127
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:35,224
rewarm your skin and blunt
the ghrelin spike.

128
00:08:35,574 --> 00:08:41,562
Follow this with a recovery meal of 30 to 40
grams of lean protein before touching

129
00:08:41,562 --> 00:08:41,995
carbs.

130
00:08:42,345 --> 00:08:46,932
On land, build the swimmer's V-taper
with a 3-day dryland blueprint.

131
00:08:47,282 --> 00:08:51,918
Day 1: pull-ups or lat pulldowns for
4 sets of 4 to 6 reps,

132
00:08:51,918 --> 00:08:56,091
straight-arm cable pulldowns, and
external rotations.

133
00:08:56,091 --> 00:09:01,191
Day 2: prone Y-T-W raises, Pallof
presses, and hollow body holds.

134
00:09:01,191 --> 00:09:07,218
Day 3: lat pulldowns with explosive
concentric intent and controlled 3-second

135
00:09:07,218 --> 00:09:11,623
eccentric return, serratus wall
slides, and hollow rocks.

136
00:09:11,973 --> 00:09:15,770
High-volume swimming places cyclic
stress on your shoulders.

137
00:09:16,121 --> 00:09:17,989
Follow four safety rules.

138
00:09:17,989 --> 00:09:24,025
First: manage volume strictly, never increasing
weekly pool yardage by more than 10

139
00:09:24,025 --> 00:09:24,671
percent.

140
00:09:24,671 --> 00:09:27,689
Second: beware of oversized hand paddles,

141
00:09:27,689 --> 00:09:33,581
which multiply torque on your shoulder joint;
use fingertip paddles until rotator

142
00:09:33,581 --> 00:09:35,449
cuff strength is verified.

143
00:09:35,799 --> 00:09:41,533
Third: practice bilateral breathing every 3
or 5 strokes to prevent asymmetric

144
00:09:41,533 --> 00:09:42,912
thoracic twisting.

145
00:09:42,912 --> 00:09:45,307
Finally: heed medical red flags.

146
00:09:45,307 --> 00:09:49,735
If you experience night pain, sharp
anterior joint catching,

147
00:09:49,735 --> 00:09:55,106
or numbness down your arm, stop swimming
and see a sports physiotherapist.

148
00:09:55,455 --> 00:10:00,447
Here is an amateur-friendly 4-day training
split that protects your tendons.

149
00:10:00,797 --> 00:10:05,067
On Monday: dryland pulling—pull-ups,
straight-arm pulldowns,

150
00:10:05,067 --> 00:10:07,658
and rotator cuff external rotations.

151
00:10:07,658 --> 00:10:12,628
On Tuesday: pool session focusing on
streamline locks, front sculling,

152
00:10:12,628 --> 00:10:14,378
and bilateral breathing.

153
00:10:14,378 --> 00:10:20,189
On Wednesday: full rest to allow shoulder
tendons and connective tissue to remodel.

154
00:10:20,539 --> 00:10:25,857
On Thursday: dryland stability—prone
Y-T-W raises, Pallof presses,

155
00:10:25,857 --> 00:10:27,682
and hollow body holds.

156
00:10:27,682 --> 00:10:33,873
On Friday: pool endurance intervals with a
pull buoy to groove Early Vertical

157
00:10:33,873 --> 00:10:35,302
Forearm mechanics.

158
00:10:35,652 --> 00:10:41,176
Take Saturday and Sunday off, saving intense
6-day splits for seasoned competitive

159
00:10:41,176 --> 00:10:41,775
swimmers.

160
00:10:42,125 --> 00:10:45,837
The iconic swimmer's V-taper is
not built by vanity.

161
00:10:46,187 --> 00:10:50,987
It is forged by the uncompromising fluid
physics of moving through water.

162
00:10:51,337 --> 00:10:54,303
Shape your hull into a zero-drag cylinder,

163
00:10:54,303 --> 00:10:58,167
vault your body forward with an
Early Vertical Forearm,

164
00:10:58,167 --> 00:11:03,134
lock your chassis with a hollow body core,
and armor your rotator cuffs.

165
00:11:03,484 --> 00:11:06,868
Build your wings with purpose,
protect your shoulders,

166
00:11:06,868 --> 00:11:08,775
and glide past the competition.
